  abstract = {Male aggression against females in primates, including humans, often\textbackslash nfunctions to control female sexuality to the male's reproductive advantage.\textbackslash nA comparative, evolutionary perspective is used to generate several\textbackslash nhypotheses to help to explain cross-cultural variation in the frequency of\textbackslash nmale aggression against women. Variables considered include protection\textbackslash nof women by kin, male--male alliances and male strategies for guarding\textbackslash nmates and obtaining adulterous matings, and male resource control. The\textbackslash nrelationships between male aggression against women and gender ideologies,\textbackslash nmale domination of women, and female sexuality are also considered.},
